TEXT 64 32 Left 2 ;CR high-pass: pulse response
TEXT 64 656 Left 2 !.tran 0 2.2m 0 1u
TEXT 64 704 Left 2 ;R1 = 1k, C1 = 100n: tau = 100 us. Each half-cycle is about 5 tau.
TEXT 64 768 Left 2 ;1. Click Run: the running-person button at the top left.
TEXT 64 808 Left 2 ;2. On the schematic, click the wire labeled VOUT (not the component).
TEXT 64 848 Left 2 ;3. Click the VIN wire too, to compare input and output in the same plot.
TEXT 64 888 Left 2 ;If the plot is empty, select the schematic window and click the labeled wire again.
TEXT 64 968 Left 2 ;EXPERIMENTS AND COMMON QUESTIONS
TEXT 64 1008 Left 2 ;Right-click a component to edit its value. Predict first, change one value, then Run.
TEXT 64 1048 Left 2 ;PLOT: VIN steps from 0 V to 5 V and back. VOUT is the voltage across R1.
TEXT 64 1088 Left 2 ;Rising edge: VOUT jumps near +5 V, then decays toward 0 V.
TEXT 64 1128 Left 2 ;Falling edge: VOUT jumps near -5 V, then decays toward 0 V again.
TEXT 64 1168 Left 2 ;WHY: Capacitor voltage cannot jump instantly. The input step first appears across R1.
TEXT 64 1208 Left 2 ;TRY: Double C1 to 200n. Predict which edges change and how long they take to decay.
TEXT 64 1248 Left 2 ;FAQ: If the pulse is shorter than a few time constants, VOUT may not settle before the next edge.
